{"causes":["Jiong of Xia rose to power after the death of his father, who was the eleventh king of the Xia dynasty.","The Xia dynasty was established in the Yellow River valley around 2100 BCE, providing Jiong with a strong foundation for his rule.","The development of bronze technology in the Xia dynasty during Jiong's reign allowed for the creation of more sophisticated tools and weapons."],"effects":["The Xia dynasty's hierarchical system of government, established during Jiong's reign, would later influence the development of Confucianism.","The growth of the Xia dynasty's power and influence during Jiong's reign laid the groundwork for the subsequent Shang dynasty.","The discovery of the Shang dynasty's oracle bones in the 19th century provided evidence of the Xia dynasty's existence and helped to solidify its place in Chinese history."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"jiong-of-xia","sourceName":"Jiong of Xia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"xia-dynasty","targetName":"Xia Dynasty","context":"Jiong of Xia was the twelfth king of the Xia dynasty."},{"sourceSlug":"jiong-of-xia","sourceName":"Jiong of Xia","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"bronze-technology","targetName":"Bronze Technology","context":"Jiong of Xia oversaw the development of bronze technology during his reign."},{"sourceSlug":"jiong-of-xia","sourceName":"Jiong of Xia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"confucianism","targetName":"Confucianism","context":"The Xia dynasty's hierarchical system of government, established during Jiong's reign, would later influence the development of Confucianism."},{"sourceSlug":"jiong-of-xia","sourceName":"Jiong of Xia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"shang-dynasty","targetName":"Shang Dynasty","context":"The growth of the Xia dynasty's power and influence during Jiong's reign laid the groundwork for the subsequent Shang dynasty."},{"sourceSlug":"jiong-of-xia","sourceName":"Jiong of Xia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"oracle-bones","targetName":"Oracle Bones","context":"The discovery of the Shang dynasty's oracle bones in the 19th century provided evidence of the Xia dynasty's existence."}],"places":[{"name":"Yellow River Valley, China","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Ancient China","role":"Region"},{"name":"China","role":"Country"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Jiong of Xia","type":"Reference article","slug":"jiong-of-xia"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q780446","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiong_of_Xia"],"tags":["Monarchs & Rulers","political-systems","empire-and-colonialism","geopolitical-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Jiong of Xia (also written Jiong; fl. c. 2000 BCE, traditional chronology) was a legendary ruler in the traditional Chinese historical sequence of the Xia dynasty — the first dynasty in Chinese historical tradition, whose historicity has been a subject of scholarly debate between those who accept the traditional accounts and those who regard the Xia as legendary. In the traditional chronology preserved in the 'Records of the Grand Historian' (Shiji) by Sima Qian (c. 100 BCE), Jiong appears as one of the later rulers of the Xia, succeeding in the dynastic line that traces back to the legendary founder Yu the Great, who is credited with taming the great floods of ancient China.

The Xia dynasty (traditionally c. 2070–1600 BCE) occupies a crucial position in Chinese historical memory: it is the dynasty that bridges the era of legendary sage-rulers (Yao, Shun, and Yu) and the historically more documented Shang dynasty (c. 1600–1046 BCE). Whether the Xia dynasty as described in Chinese historical texts represents an actual Bronze Age polity, a legendary confederation of Neolithic cultures, or a later invention of Zhou-era historiography remains one of the central debates in Chinese archaeology. The discovery and excavation of Erlitou culture sites (c. 1900–1500 BCE) in the Yellow River valley — with their palace complexes, bronze artefacts, and signs of political centralisation — has led many scholars to identify Erlitou as the Xia civilisation, though the identification remains contested.

Jiong of Xia, like most rulers in the traditional Xia king list, is a name without independently datable documentation — his existence depends on the accuracy of the traditional historical lists transmitted through Chinese court historiography. His significance lies primarily as an entry in the record of China's claimed dynastic continuity — the unbroken sequence of dynasties from Xia through Shang, Zhou, and beyond that was central to Chinese political legitimacy and historical consciousness.","subjects":["Ancient China","Chinese Dynasties","Classical Era","Bronze Age","Chinese History","Ancient History","Xia Dynasty","Chinese Historiography"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Jiong of Xia's reign marked an important period in the development of the Xia dynasty, which would go on to shape Chinese history for centuries to come.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
